          "title": "Fundamentals - Lesson 1",
          "content": "## Fundamentals - Lesson 1\n\n*Content generation had an error. Raw output preserved below for debugging.*\n\n```\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"what-is-system-design\",\n  \"slug\": \"what-is-system-design\",\n  \"title\": \"What Is System Design, Really?\",\n  \"content\": \"## What You'll Learn\\n- The difference between \\\"coding\\\" and \\\"designing\\\" a system\\n- Why every line of code is a design decision with 10× downstream cost\\n- The three lenses you must look through before you ship: scalability, reliability, cost\\n\\n## Concept Explanation\\nWriting code is easy. Designing a system that won’t collapse when your product hits the front page of Hacker News—that’s the hard part. System design is the deliberate act of turning fuzzy product requirements into a concrete blueprint of services, data flows, and trade-offs that can be built, operated, and evolved by a team you haven’t even hired yet.\\n\\nThink of it like architecture for software. An architect doesn’t just draw pretty façades; she calculates load-bearing walls, fire-escape routes, and where the plumbing will run twenty years from now. In our world the walls are APIs, the plumbing is event streams, and the fire escape is your rollback strategy.\\n\\nAt the staff-plus level you are no longer paid for lines of code—you are paid for decisions that save (or make) the company millions. A single early choice—say, synchronous REST vs. async events—can determine whether you scale to 10 M DAU with 20 micro-services or 200 micro-services and a 40-person on-call rotation. <!-- voice:key_insight -->\\n\\n## How It Works\\nLet’s make this concrete. Below is a **design snippet**, not code. It describes two alternate ways to let users “like” a tweet. Both work on day one; only one survives day 1000.\\n\\n```python\\n# Design A: Synchronous monolith\\nPOST /likes\\n  - API gateway → Like-Service (EC2)\\n  - SQL INSERT into `likes` table (RDS)\\n  - UPDATE `tweets.like_count` (same transaction)\\n  - 201 OK returned to client\\n\\n# Design B: Event-driven\\nPOST /likes\\n  - API gateway → Like-Service (container)\\n  - Kafka event: LikeCreated {user_id, tweet_id}\\n  - Like-Service\n```"
