          "id": "fundamentals-lesson-2",
          "slug": "fundamentals-lesson-2",
          "title": "System Design North Star: The Three Universal Truths",
          "content": "## What You'll Learn\n- The three questions that decide every design trade-off\n- How to translate business speak into engineering constraints\n- A 90-second mental checklist used by senior architects at Google, Netflix, and Uber\n\n<!-- voice:section_check -->\n\n### The North Star Framework\n\nEvery system design decision—whether you're choosing a database, drawing boxes, or estimating cost—boils down to three universal truths:\n\n1. **Traffic Truth**: How many requests per second will hit this thing?  \n2. **Latency Truth**: How fast must it respond (p50, p99, p99.9)?  \n3. **Durability Truth**: How much data loss or downtime is survivable?  \n\nIf you can answer those three numbers, 80 % of the “which tech should we use?” debate disappears.  \n\n### From Business Requirement to Engineering Number\n\nStakeholders rarely hand you neat metrics. Instead you hear:\n> “We need it to be fast and never go down.”\n\nYour job is to translate poetry into physics.  \n\n| Business Poetry | Engineering Physics |\n|-----------------|---------------------|\n| “We’re launching globally next quarter” | 1 M DAU → ~11.6 RPS sustained, 100 RPS peak |\n| “Users hate waiting” | p99 < 200 ms for reads, p99 < 500 ms for writes |\n| “Losing orders costs millions” | RPO ≤ 1 min, RTO ≤ 5 min |\n\n<!-- voice:key_insight -->\n\n### The 90-Second Checklist\nBefore you draw the first box, say these out loud:\n1. “We will see **X** requests per second at peak.”  \n2. “The 99-th percentile latency must stay under **Y** ms.”  \n3. “We can survive **Z** minutes of downtime and **W** minutes of data loss.”  \n\nIf any of those variables is missing, stop designing and start asking.\n\n### Common Patterns\n- **E-commerce checkout**: 1 k RPS, p99 < 300 ms, RPO = 0, RTO < 2 min  \n- **Social feed**: 50 k RPS, p99 < 100 ms, RPO = 5 min, RTO < 10 min  \n- **Analytics ingestion**: 10 k RPS, p99 < 2 s, RPO = 15 min, RTO < 30 min  \n\n### Watch Out For\n- **The “infinite scale” trap**—a vague “we’ll be huge” with no numbers.  \n- **Latency inflation**—forgetting that p99 is 4× slower than average.  \n- **Hidden durability**—backup windows that exceed your stated RPO.\n\n### Try It Yourself\nYou are designing the order-processing service for a flash-sale app.  \nThe product manager says:  \n> “We expect 200 k concurrent users on launch day; nobody should wait more than a second, and losing even one paid order is unacceptable.”\n\nTurn that into the three universal truths.  \nWrite the three numbers you would walk into the design review with.",
          "starterCode": "# TODO: Fill in the three universal truths for the flash-sale scenario.\n# Return a dict with keys: traffic_rps, latency_p99_ms, durability_rpo_min.\n\ndef flash_sale_truths():\n    # TODO: Convert the PM statement into engineering numbers\n    # Hint: 200 k concurrent users ≠ 200 k RPS. Think about click-through rate.\n    pass\n\n# Test cases — do not modify\nans = flash_sale_truths()\nassert 25000 <= ans['traffic_rps'] <= 35000, \"traffic_rps should be ~30 k peak\"\nassert ans['latency_p99_ms'] <= 1000, \"latency must be ≤ 1 s\"\nassert ans['durability_rpo_min'] == 0, \"zero order loss means zero RPO\"\nprint(\"All assertions passed!\")",
          "solutionCode": "def flash_sale_truths():\n    # 200 k concurrent users, 10 % hit checkout in any given minute\n    # Peak surge factor 2× → 200 000 * 0.1 / 60 * 2 ≈ 667 ops/sec per action\n    # Assume 5 checkout steps (add-to-cart, address, payment, confirm, callback)\n    # 667 * 5 ≈ 3.3 k RPS sustained; peak 10× for launch minute → 30 k RPS\n    traffic_rps = 30000\n\n    # PM said “nobody should wait more than a second” → p99 < 1 s\n    latency_p99_ms = 1000\n\n    # “Losing even one paid order is unacceptable” → zero data loss\n    durability_rpo_min = 0\n\n    return {\n        'traffic_rps': traffic_rps,\n        'latency_p99_ms': latency_p99_ms,\n        'durability_rpo_min': durability_rpo_min\n    }\n\n# Test cases\nans = flash_sale_truths()\nassert 25000 <= ans['traffic_rps'] <= 35000, \"traffic_rps should be ~30 k peak\"\nassert ans['latency_p99_ms'] <= 1000, \"latency must be ≤ 1 s\"\nassert ans['durability_rpo_min'] == 0, \"zero order loss means zero RPO\"\nprint(\"All assertions passed!\")"
