          "id": "availability-vs-consistency",
          "slug": "availability-vs-consistency",
          "title": "Availability vs. Consistency: Pick One",
          "content": "## Availability vs. Consistency: Pick One\n\n<!-- voice:section_check -->\n\n### What You'll Learn\n- The formal CAP theorem definition (and why it matters)\n- How to detect which side of the trade-off a system chose\n- A 3-step checklist to justify your choice in design reviews\n- When you can cheat and get both (spoiler: not often)\n\n### The Moment You Can't Un-See\nPicture this: your payment service is up, but the database says your balance is null. You can either show the user a spinner (and risk a timeout) or show $0.00 (and risk an overdraft). Picking one feels like choosing which of your kids gets the last oxygen mask.<!-- voice:key_insight -->\n\nThat is the availability-vs-consistency decision. Every distributed system makes it, whether the designer admits it or not.\n\n### CAP in One Breath\nEric Brewer’s CAP theorem (proved by Gilbert & Lynch, 2002) says:\n\n> **C**onsistency: every read receives the most recent write or an error.  \n> **A**vailability: every request receives a (non-error) response, without the guarantee that it contains the most recent write.  \n> **P**artition tolerance: the system continues to operate despite arbitrary messages being dropped or delayed by the network.  \n\nPick two. But since partitions *happen*, the real choice is only between CP and AP.\n\n### How to Spot the Trade-Off in the Wild\nLook at the *first* thing the system does when a partition appears:\n\n- **CP systems** (e.g., ZooKeeper, etcd, HBase) **fail the request** if they cannot talk to a majority. They’d rather be unavailable than lie.\n- **AP systems** (e.g., Cassandra, DynamoDB with eventual consistency, S3) **answer immediately**, possibly with stale data, and repair later.\n\n### A 30-Second Design-Review Script\nSenior engineers don’t debate CAP in the abstract; they nail the *decision record*:\n\n1. **State the worst-case user pain** if we are unavailable for 30 s.  \n2. **State the worst-case user pain** if we serve stale data for 30 s.  \n3. **Pick the smaller pain**, write it in the ADR, and add a monitoring alert on the metric you sacrificed.\n\nThat is it. No quorum math required in the meeting.\n\n### When You Can (Sort of) Get Both\n- **PAUSED writes**: accept writes during partition, but block *reads* of affected keys until the partition heals (DynamoDB “read-after-write” on a single item).\n- **CRDTs / convergent data types**: guarantee that *eventually* all replicas agree without conflicts, but you must design your data model around them (counters, sets, maps).\n- **Tiered guarantees**: financial ledger is CP, user session is AP; same product, two subsystems, two choices.\n\n### Watch Out For\n1. **“Strong consistency” marketing**—look for the fine print: single-region only, or single-key only, or 99.9 % SLA, not 100 %.\n2. **Forgetting to fail forward**: if you pick AP, you *must* build a reconciliation job and an alert that fires when divergence exceeds X.\n3. **Confusing latency with availability**: a 200 ms response that is stale is still “available”; a 30 s timeout is *unavailable*.\n\n### Try It Yourself\nYou are designing the inventory service for a flash-sale event. The marketing team promises 10 000 hot-selling items. During checkout we must decrement stock, but the database is split across two data centers. Implement two tiny functions—one CP, one AP—and measure how many checkouts succeed when we kill one DC.\n\n<!-- voice:section_check -->",
          "starterCode": "# TODO: Implement two inventory decrement strategies\n# 1. cp_checkout()  - blocks until quorum is reached or raises\n# 2. ap_checkout()  - always answers, may oversell\n# You may simulate the network partition by raising NetworkPartition\n\nclass NetworkPartition(Exception):\n    pass\n\ndef cp_checkout(stock_table, item_id, qty):\n    \"\"\"\n    Strongly consistent checkout.\n    If a partition prevents a quorum write, raise NetworkPartition.\n    Otherwise return True and the new stock level.\n    \"\"\"\n    # TODO: your code here\n    pass\n\ndef ap_checkout(stock_table, item_id, qty):\n    \"\"\"\n    Available checkout.\n    Always returns immediately with (success:bool, new_stock:int).\n    success=False means insufficient stock; new_stock may be stale.\n    \"\"\"\n    # TODO: your code here\n    pass\n\n# ---- test harness ----\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    stock = {\"widget\": 5}\n    try:\n        ok, new = cp_checkout(stock, \"widget\", 1)\n        print(\"CP success:\", ok, \"stock:\", new)\n    except NetworkPartition:\n        print(\"CP blocked by partition\")\n\n    ok, new = ap_checkout(stock, \"widget\", 1)\n    print(\"AP success:\", ok, \"stock:\", new)",
          "solutionCode": "class NetworkPartition(Exception):\n    pass\n\n# Simulated cluster state for demo\n_cluster_partitioned = False\n\ndef cp_checkout(stock_table, item_id, qty):\n    \"\"\"CP path: refuse to proceed without quorum.\"\"\"\n    if _cluster_partitioned:\n        raise NetworkPartition(\"no quorum\")\n    current = stock_table[item_id]\n    if current < qty:\n        return False, current\n    stock_table[item_id] -= qty\n    return True, stock_table[item_id]\n\ndef ap_checkout(stock_table, item_id, qty):\n    \"\"\"AP path: always answer with local state.\"\"\"\n    current = stock_table[item_id]  # may be stale\n    if current < qty:\n        return False, current\n    stock_table[item_id] -= qty\n    return True, stock_table[item_id]\n\n# ---- test harness ----\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    stock = {\"widget\": 5}\n    try:\n        ok, new = cp_checkout(stock, \"widget\", 1)\n        print(\"CP success:\", ok, \"stock:\", new)\n    except NetworkPartition:\n        print(\"CP blocked by partition\")\n\n    ok, new = ap_checkout(stock, \"widget\", 1)\n    print(\"AP success:\", ok, \"stock:\", new)"
