          "slug": "scalability-lesson-1",
          "title": "Scalability - Lesson 1",
          "content": "## Scalability - Lesson 1\n\n*Content generation had an error. Raw output preserved below for debugging.*\n\n```\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"scalability-basics\",\n  \"slug\": \"scalability-basics\",\n  \"title\": \"Scalability Basics: From 1 RPS to 1 M RPS\",\n  \"content\": \"## What You'll Learn\\n- The formal definition of scalability and why \\\"it works at small scale\\\" is never enough\\n- How to read and sketch a load-vs-latency curve to spot cliffs before production\\n- When to choose vertical vs. horizontal scaling (and when you really need both)\\n\\n<!-- voice:section_check --> Let's make sure we're on the same page: scalability is the *capability* of a system to keep correct behaviour and acceptable latency *while* the load grows. It is not speed, it is not throughput, it is the *shape* of the curve.\\n\\n## Concept Explanation\\nImagine you own a food-truck that serves the best tacos in town. One rainy Tuesday you get 10 customers/hour and every order is ready in 30 s. On Saturday the local soccer team wins; 1 000 hungry fans show up. If you can still hand out tacos in ~30 s, your *taco-system* scaled. If the line backs up for two blocks and people leave, it didn't.\\n\\nIn distributed systems we draw that story as a load-vs-latency curve. The x-axis is offered load (requests per second, messages per second, GB/s, ...). The y-axis is the 99th-percentile latency you promise in the SLA. The curve stays flat, then somewhere it bends upward; that bend is the *knee* and the x-coordinate of the knee is your *scalability limit*. Your job is to push the knee as far right as budget and physics allow.\\n\\nThe curve is shaped by three *scalability killers*:\\n1. Shared mutable state protected by a single lock (the food truck has one grill)\\n2. Unbounded fan-in or fan-out (one cashier, no queueing)\\n3. Resource exhaustion that is *coordinated* across nodes (all instances hit the same 2 TB DB partition)\\n\\nGood designs convert coordinated exhaustion into *uncoordinated* local exhaustion, which is easier to fix.\\n\\n### Vertical vs. Horizontal\\n**Vertical scaling (scaling-up)** means giving the existing node more CP\n```"
