          "id": "scalability-lesson-4",
          "slug": "scalability-lesson-4",
          "title": "Scalability - Lesson 4",
          "content": "## Scalability - Lesson 4\n\n*Content generation had an error. Raw output preserved below for debugging.*\n\n```\n```json\n{\n  \"id\": \"cache-eviction-policies\",\n  \"slug\": \"cache-eviction-policies\",\n  \"title\": \"Cache Eviction Policies: LRU, LFU, FIFO — When & Why\",\n  \"content\": \"## What You'll Learn\\n- How modern caches decide what to evict when memory is full\\n- The trade-offs between recency, frequency, and simplicity\\n- A production-ready LRU implementation you can drop into any service\\n\\n## Why Eviction Matters\\nImagine your recommendation service keeps the top 10k products in Redis. At 3 A.M. Black-Friday traffic hits, memory fills, and Redis starts evicting keys. If it evicts the wrong ones, every missed cache hit becomes an expensive database round-trip, and your p99 latency spikes from 5 ms to 800 ms. The policy you choose determines whether your cache is a hero or a liability.\\n\\n## The Three Classic Policies\\n\\n### 1. FIFO (First-In, First-Out)\\nA queue. When the cache is full, the oldest inserted item is evicted.\\n- **Pros**: O(1) insert/evict, trivial to implement.\\n- **Cons**: Zero intelligence; a hot item that arrived first is evicted just because it’s old.\\n- **Use when**: Data has natural lifetimes (e.g., daily price snapshots).\\n\\n### 2. LFU (Least-Frequently-Used)\\nA min-heap keyed by access count. Evict the item with the smallest count.\\n- **Pros**: Keeps “popular” items.\\n- **Cons**: Counts can become stale; a viral tweet stays forever even after interest dies.\\n- **Use when**: Access patterns are stable (e.g., top-traded stocks on a weekday).\\n\\n### 3. LRU (Least-Recently-Used)\\nA linked list ordered by recency. Evict the tail (least recent).\\n- **Pros**: Adapts quickly to shifting workloads; most common in production.\\n- **Cons**: Slight overhead; hash map + doubly-linked list.\\n- **Use when**: Workload has temporal locality (99 % of real systems).\\n\\n<!-- voice:section_check -->\\n**Quick check**: If your cache suddenly sees a new trending hashtag, which policy keeps it? (Answer: LRU, because recency trumps accumulated counts in bursty traffic.)\\n\\n## Buildi\n```"
