          "title": "YouTube: Planet-Scale Video Metadata Sharding",
          "content": "## What You'll Learn\n- How YouTube shards exabyte-scale video metadata across regions\n- The “generate-and-test” ID scheme that eliminates global coordination\n- Why YouTube moved from MySQL-primary to Spanner-global SQL\n- How to size shards for 100 M writes/day with 5-second global consistency\n\n<!-- voice:key_insight -->\n\n## The Situation\nIn 2023 YouTube crossed 2.7 billion monthly users uploading 3.7 M videos per day. Each upload touches at least six metadata tables: videos, channels, playlists, comments, captions, and user-history. At peak (Super-Bowl Sunday) the cluster sustained 1.2 M writes/s with a p99 commit latency < 200 ms while remaining strongly consistent across 18 Google regions.\n\nThe business constraint: any view-count or comment must be immediately consistent for ad-billing and creator analytics. Losing even 0.01 % of writes costs real money.\n\n<!-- voice:section_check -->",
